M10 = {"id": "IL26-M10", "patch": {
 "objectives": [
  "Distinguish populations from samples and explain sampling bias, survivorship bias, and selection effects in business data.",
  "Apply the Central Limit Theorem to justify confidence intervals for means and interpret them without the classic misreadings.",
  "Choose between z, t, and bootstrap intervals based on sample size and distribution shape, and compute them.",
  "Explain correlation vs causation, confounding, and Simpson's paradox with business examples."],
 "notes": [
  {"title": "Populations, samples, and the biases that eat analyses",
   "body": "A population is everything; a sample is what you measured. Inference generalizes from sample to population — valid only if the sample represents the population. Survivorship bias (analyzing only survivors), selection bias (non-random inclusion), and convenience sampling are how business data lies.\n\nWorked example (with solution):\n```text\n'Our app-store rating rose from 4.1 to 4.6 after the redesign!'\nBias audit: ratings are VOLUNTARY. After the redesign, unhappy users churned\ninstead of rating (survivorship), and promoters were prompted to rate\n(selection). The 4.6 describes 'users who stayed AND rated', not 'users'.\nHonest version: report rating among raters + churn rate + a prompted survey\nof a random sample. Three numbers, not one.\n```"},
  {"title": "The Central Limit Theorem: why averages behave",
   "body": "CLT: the sampling distribution of the mean approaches normal as n grows, regardless of the data's shape, with standard error = s/sqrt(n). This justifies confidence intervals for means even with skewed data — given enough n. Standard error shrinks with sqrt(n): 4x the data halves the error.\n\nWorked example (with solution):\n```python\n# 95% CI for mean order value: n=400, mean=$52, s=$20.\nimport math\nn, mean, s = 400, 52, 20\nse = s / math.sqrt(n)          # 1.0\nmargin = 1.96 * se             # 1.96\nprint(f'95% CI: (${mean-margin:.2f}, ${mean+margin:.2f})')  # ($50.04, $53.96)\n# To halve the margin: need 4x the sample (n=1600), since SE ~ 1/sqrt(n).\n```"},
  {"title": "Confidence intervals without the misreadings",
   "body": "A 95% CI means: over repeated sampling, 95% of such intervals contain the true parameter. It does NOT mean '95% chance the true value is inside this interval' (the parameter is fixed; the interval is random). Wider CI = more uncertainty; a CI excluding zero for a difference = significant at 5%.\n\nWorked example (with solution):\n```text\nConversion lift CI: (+0.2%, +1.8%). Correct reads:\n- 'We are 95% confident the true lift is between 0.2 and 1.8 points.'\n- The interval excludes 0 -> statistically significant at alpha=0.05.\n- Width (1.6 points) quantifies uncertainty: the lift could be tiny or solid.\nWRONG reads: 'There's a 95% chance the lift is in here' (parameter isn't\nrandom); 'The lift is probably 1.0%' (the center is the point estimate,\nnot the most probable value).\n```"},
  {"title": "z vs t vs bootstrap",
   "body": "z-interval: large n or known sigma. t-interval: small n, roughly normal data (heavier tails account for estimated sigma). Bootstrap: resample the data with replacement thousands of times; take percentiles — no normality needed, works for medians and other statistics.\n\nWorked example (with solution):\n```python\n# Bootstrap 95% CI for the MEDIAN (no formula exists for skewed data):\nimport numpy as np\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\ndata = rng.exponential(scale=100, size=200) + 20  # skewed session lengths\nboots = [np.median(rng.choice(data, size=len(data), replace=True)) for _ in range(5000)]\nlo, hi = np.percentile(boots, [2.5, 97.5])\nprint(f'95% bootstrap CI for median: ({lo:.1f}, {hi:.1f})')\n# Compare: the t-interval for the MEAN would be misleading here (skew + n=200\n# is okay-ish, but the median is the honest center: bootstrap handles it).\n```"},
  {"title": "Correlation, causation, confounding, Simpson",
   "body": "Correlation measures linear association (-1 to 1); it never proves causation. Confounders drive both variables (ice cream and drowning: summer heat). Simpson's paradox: a trend reverses within subgroups — the aggregate lies because subgroup mix differs.\n\nWorked example (with solution):\n```text\nOverall: Feature users convert at 12% vs 8% for non-users. 'The feature works!'\nSplit by tenure:\n  New users:    feature 5% vs no-feature 6%  (feature WORSE)\n  Tenured users: feature 20% vs no-feature 18% (feature slightly better)\nSimpson's paradox: feature adoption correlates with tenure, and tenure drives\nconversion. The aggregate compares mostly-tenured feature users against\nmostly-new non-users. Correct analysis: compare WITHIN tenure bands, or\nmodel with tenure as a covariate. Never ship the aggregate claim.\n```"}],
 "cards": [
  {"front": "Survivorship bias in one sentence?", "back": "Analyzing only survivors (stayers, raters, winners) and generalizing to everyone."},
  {"front": "CLT in one sentence?", "back": "Sample means approach normality as n grows; SE = s/sqrt(n)."},
  {"front": "n=400, mean=52, s=20. 95% CI?", "back": "SE=1.0, margin=1.96 -> ($50.04, $53.96)."},
  {"front": "To halve a confidence interval's width, sample size must?", "back": "Quadruple (SE shrinks with 1/sqrt(n))."},
  {"front": "Correct reading of a 95% CI?", "back": "95% of such intervals from repeated sampling contain the true parameter."},
  {"front": "t-interval vs z-interval?", "back": "t for small n with estimated sigma (heavier tails); z for large n/known sigma."},
  {"front": "When is bootstrap the right tool?", "back": "Non-normal data or statistics without formulas (e.g., medians): resample and take percentiles."},
  {"front": "Simpson's paradox?", "back": "Aggregate trend reverses within subgroups due to differing subgroup mix — always check within-group."}],
 "quiz": [
  {"q": "App rating rose 4.1 -> 4.6 after a redesign, but ratings are voluntary and churn rose. The threat?", "choices": ["No threat; ratings are ratings.", "Sample size too small.", "Survivorship + selection bias: the 4.6 describes stayers who rated, not all users.", "Regression to the mean."], "answer": 2, "explain": "Unhappy users churned instead of rating; the metric conditions on staying and rating."},
  {"q": "n=400, mean=$52, s=$20. 95% CI for the mean?", "choices": ["($50.04, $53.96)", "($32, $72)", "($51, $53)", "($48, $56)"], "answer": 0, "explain": "SE = 20/20 = 1.0; margin = 1.96 -> ($50.04, $53.96)."},
  {"q": "Which reading of '95% CI (0.2%, 1.8%)' is correct?", "choices": ["95% chance the true lift is inside.", "Over repeated sampling, 95% of such intervals contain the true lift; it excludes 0 so it is significant at 5%.", "The lift is probably 1.0%.", "The lift is exactly 1.8%."], "answer": 1, "explain": "CIs are about the procedure's long-run coverage, not a probability on the fixed parameter."},
  {"q": "To halve the margin of error, you need:", "choices": ["Double the sample", "Half the sample", "10x the sample", "4x the sample"], "answer": 3, "explain": "SE ~ 1/sqrt(n); halving SE needs 4x n."},
  {"q": "Small sample (n=15), roughly normal data, sigma unknown. Interval?", "choices": ["z-interval", "No interval possible", "Bootstrap only", "t-interval"], "answer": 3, "explain": "t accounts for the extra uncertainty of estimating sigma from a small sample."},
  {"q": "Feature users convert 12% vs 8%, but within tenure bands the feature is flat or worse. This is:", "choices": ["Proof the feature works", "Simpson's paradox: tenure confounds the aggregate", "A calculation error", "Regression to the mean"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Adoption correlates with tenure; the aggregate compares different mixes. Analyze within bands."},
  {"q": "Bootstrap CI for the median: procedure?", "choices": ["Resample with replacement thousands of times, take 2.5/97.5 percentiles of medians", "Assume normality", "Use the t-table", "Double the sample"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Bootstrapping builds the sampling distribution empirically - no normality needed."},
  {"q": "Ice cream sales correlate with drowning deaths. Correct conclusion?", "choices": ["Ban ice cream", "More lifeguards reduce ice cream sales", "Confounding by summer heat: correlation is not causation", "The correlation is spurious and useless"], "answer": 2, "explain": "A third variable (heat/season) drives both; correlation alone never establishes causation."},],
 "drills": [{"id": "il26-m10-stats", "title": "Inference drills", "items": [
   {"prompt": "SE of the mean: n=100, s=30?", "answer": "3.0", "explain": "30/sqrt(100) = 3."},
   {"prompt": "95% margin with SE=3?", "answer": "5.88", "explain": "1.96*3 = 5.88."},
   {"prompt": "Name two sampling biases.", "answer": "Survivorship, selection (also: convenience, non-response)", "explain": "Non-random inclusion mechanisms."},
   {"prompt": "CI (2%, 8%) for a difference: significant at 5%?", "answer": "Yes (excludes 0)", "explain": "Excluding the null value = reject at alpha=0.05."},
   {"prompt": "CI (-1%, 3%): significant?", "answer": "No (includes 0)", "explain": "Cannot reject the null of no difference."},
   {"prompt": "Correlation = 0.9 proves?", "answer": "Strong linear association; proves nothing about causation", "explain": "Correlation never implies causation."}]}],
 "handsOnLab": {"id": "il26-m10-lab", "title": "CI and bias audit lab", "brief": "Compute z, t, and bootstrap intervals on real skewed data, compare them, and write a bias audit of a vanity metric.",
  "steps": ["Compute the 95% z-interval for mean session length (n=200, skewed).", "Compute the t-interval; note how little changes at n=200.", "Bootstrap the CI for the median; compare width and center to the mean intervals.", "Pick the honest interval for a stakeholder and justify the choice.", "Write the bias audit: three ways the 'average rating rose' headline could be survivorship/selection."],
  "deliverable": "Three intervals with comparison, justified choice, bias audit memo.",
  "checkpoint": "Bootstrap median CI computed from ≥1000 resamples; the bias audit names mechanisms, not vibes."},
 "mockTask": {"id": "il26-m10-mock", "type": "choice", "q": "A vendor claims 'users who enable notifications retain 2x better.' What is the analyst's first move?", "choices": ["Recommend forcing notifications on.", "Check for confounding/self-selection: engaged users enable notifications; compare within engagement bands or run an experiment before claiming causation.", "Publish the 2x figure.", "Ask for more data."], "answer": 1, "explain": "Opt-in features carry massive self-selection bias. The 2x is correlation until a within-band comparison or experiment says otherwise."},
 "crossLinks": [{"course": "il-20-mathematics", "moduleId": "m20-statistics", "topic": "Statistics theory", "why": "IL-20 teaches the theory; this module applies it to analyst inference."}],
 "videoPlaceholders": False}}

