M11 = {"id": "IL26-M11", "patch": {
 "objectives": [
  "State null and alternative hypotheses for business questions and choose the right test (t, proportions, chi-square) for the data type.",
  "Compute p-values and confidence intervals for A/B tests and explain what they do and do not prove.",
  "Design A/B tests: randomization, sample-size planning via power analysis, guardrail metrics, and minimum detectable effects.",
  "Detect and avoid peeking, multiple-comparison inflation, novelty effects, and other experiment killers."],
 "notes": [
  {"title": "Hypotheses, p-values, and what 'significant' means",
   "body": "H0 (null): no effect. H1 (alternative): there is an effect. The p-value is P(data this extreme | H0 true) — NOT P(H0 true | data). p < 0.05 means 'surprising under the null,' not 'the effect is real and large.' Statistical significance != practical significance.\n\nWorked example (with solution):\n```python\n# Two-proportion z-test: control 120/1000 converted, variant 150/1000.\nimport math\np1, p2, n1, n2 = 120/1000, 150/1000, 1000, 1000\np_pool = (120+150)/(n1+n2)\nse = math.sqrt(p_pool*(1-p_pool)*(1/n1 + 1/n2))\nz = (p2-p1)/se\nprint('z =', round(z, 2))  # z = 1.96 -> p ~= 0.05, just significant\n# Lift: 3.0 percentage points, 25% relative. Report BOTH the p-value\n# and the effect size: 'significant but is +3pp worth shipping?'\n```"},
  {"title": "Designing A/B tests: power and sample size",
   "body": "Power = P(detecting a real effect), typically targeted at 80%. Sample size depends on: baseline rate, minimum detectable effect (MDE), power, and alpha. Smaller MDE -> much larger n. Pre-register the primary metric, the MDE, and the runtime before launching.\n\nWorked example (with solution):\n```text\nBaseline conversion 10%, MDE 2 percentage points (10% -> 12%), alpha 0.05, power 0.8.\nPer-variant n ~= 16 * p(1-p) / delta^2  (rule-of-thumb for proportions)\n           ~= 16 * 0.10*0.90 / 0.02^2 = 16 * 0.09 / 0.0004 = 3,600 per variant.\nAt 1,000 visitors/day/variant: ~4 days minimum runtime.\nDesign sins: launching without this math ('we'll watch it'), stopping early\nwhen it 'looks good' (peeking inflates false positives), changing the\nprimary metric mid-flight.\n```"},
  {"title": "Peeking, multiple comparisons, and novelty effects",
   "body": "Peeking (checking significance daily and stopping when p<0.05) can inflate the false-positive rate several-fold. Multiple comparisons: testing 20 metrics at 5% guarantees ~1 false 'win' on average — correct with Bonferroni or pre-registered primaries. Novelty/primacy effects fade: run long enough to see the steady state.\n\nWorked example (with solution):\n```text\nA team checks their test every morning for 14 days, stopping the first day\np < 0.05. Simulation truth: NO real effect.\nResult: they 'find significance' ~30-40% of the time (not 5%).\nFixes: (a) fixed-horizon design: decide n upfront, look once;\n(b) sequential methods (alpha-spending) if you must peek;\n(c) separate primary metric (decision) from secondary metrics (learning).\nNovelty check: a UI change wins week 1 (+8%) then regresses to +1% by week 3:\nship only if the effect survives the novelty window.\n```"},
  {"title": "Beyond basic A/B: CUPED, stratification, guardrails",
   "body": "CUPED (Controlled-experiment Using Pre-Experiment Data) uses pre-period covariates to cut variance — same power with smaller samples. Stratified randomization balances key segments. Guardrail metrics (latency, error rate, revenue per user) catch harm the primary metric misses.\n\nWorked example (with solution):\n```python\n# CUPED intuition: adjust post-period metric by pre-period behavior.\n# Y_adj = Y_post - theta * (X_pre - mean(X_pre)), theta = cov(Y,X)/var(X).\n# If pre-period spend predicts post-period spend well, variance drops sharply.\nimport numpy as np\nrng = np.random.default_rng(1)\npre = rng.normal(100, 15, 5000)\npost = 0.9*pre + rng.normal(5, 10, 5000)  # strong pre/post correlation\ntheta = np.cov(post, pre)[0,1] / np.var(pre)\nadj = post - theta*(pre - pre.mean())\nprint('var raw:', round(np.var(post),1), ' var CUPED:', round(np.var(adj),1))\n# Variance roughly halves: same MDE detectable with ~half the sample.\n```"},
  {"title": "Communicating results: the experiment memo",
   "body": "Every experiment ends in a memo: hypothesis, design (metric, MDE, n, runtime), result (effect + CI + p), guardrails, segment cuts, and the SHIP/HOLD/ITERATE recommendation with the business reasoning. A significant p-value with a trivial effect is a HOLD.\n\nWorked example (with solution):\n```text\nMEMO: Checkout button test (14 days, n=7,200/variant)\nHypothesis: green button increases purchase conversion.\nResult: +0.3pp (10.0% -> 10.3%), 95% CI (-0.4pp, +1.0pp), p = 0.38.\nGuardrails: latency unchanged; revenue/user flat.\nSegment: mobile +0.8pp (p=0.09), desktop -0.2pp — not significant, hypothesis only.\nRECOMMENDATION: HOLD. No evidence of effect; the CI rules out our 2pp MDE.\nDo not re-run 'until it works' — that is p-hacking with extra steps.\n```"}],
 "cards": [
  {"front": "p-value definition (exact)?", "back": "P(data this extreme | H0 true). Not P(H0 is true)."},
  {"front": "Control 120/1000, variant 150/1000. z?", "back": "z = 1.96, p ~= 0.05 — just significant."},
  {"front": "Sample-size rule of thumb for proportions?", "back": "n ~= 16*p(1-p)/delta^2 per variant."},
  {"front": "Why is peeking dangerous?", "back": "Repeated significance checks inflate the false-positive rate far above 5%."},
  {"front": "Multiple comparisons fix?", "back": "Pre-register one primary metric; Bonferroni or FDR control for the rest."},
  {"front": "CUPED in one sentence?", "back": "Use pre-experiment covariates to cut variance — same power, smaller sample."},
  {"front": "Guardrail metrics are for?", "back": "Catching harm (latency, errors, revenue/user) the primary metric misses."},
  {"front": "Significant p-value, trivial effect: decision?", "back": "HOLD — statistical significance is not practical significance."}],
 "quiz": [
  {"q": "The p-value is:", "choices": ["P(H0 is true)", "P(H1 is true)", "The probability the effect is large", "P(data this extreme | H0 true)"], "answer": 3, "explain": "The p-value conditions on the null being true; it is not a probability about the hypotheses."},
  {"q": "Checking significance daily and stopping at the first p<0.05:", "choices": ["Is best practice", "Inflates false positives far above 5%", "Increases power", "Is required"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Peeking turns a 5% test into a 30-40% false-positive machine; use fixed horizons or sequential methods."},
  {"q": "Baseline 10%, MDE 2pp, alpha 0.05, power 0.8. Per-variant n ~?", "choices": ["3,600", "500", "10,000", "1,000"], "answer": 0, "explain": "16*0.09/0.0004 = 3,600 per variant."},
  {"q": "Testing 20 secondary metrics at 5% without correction: expected false wins?", "choices": ["0", "10", "About 1", "20"], "answer": 2, "explain": "20 * 0.05 = 1 expected false positive under the global null."},
  {"q": "CUPED reduces required sample size by:", "choices": ["Increasing alpha", "Using pre-experiment covariates to cut variance", "Peeking", "Dropping guardrails"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Adjusting for pre-period behavior shrinks variance; same MDE needs less n."},
  {"q": "A UI change wins week 1 (+8%) then fades to +1% by week 3. This is:", "choices": ["Proof of success", "A bug", "Seasonality", "A novelty effect: ship only if the effect survives the novelty window"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Novelty/primacy effects inflate early results; run to steady state."},
  {"q": "Result: +0.3pp, 95% CI (-0.4pp, +1.0pp), p=0.38, MDE was 2pp. Decision?", "choices": ["Ship it", "HOLD: no evidence; CI rules out the MDE", "Re-run until significant", "Change the metric"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Non-significant and the CI excludes the 2pp MDE: the hoped-for effect is ruled out. HOLD."},
  {"q": "Guardrail metrics in an experiment are:", "choices": ["Metrics like latency/error rate that must not degrade", "The primary success metric", "Vanity metrics", "Segment cuts"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Guardrails catch harm the primary metric misses; they can veto a ship."},],
 "drills": [{"id": "il26-m11-ab", "title": "A/B testing drills", "items": [
   {"prompt": "H0 for a button-color test?", "answer": "No difference in conversion between colors", "explain": "The null is always 'no effect'."},
   {"prompt": "Power = ?", "answer": "P(detecting a real effect), usually targeted at 80%", "explain": "1 - beta."},
   {"prompt": "Alpha = 0.05 means?", "answer": "5% false-positive rate under the null", "explain": "The significance threshold."},
   {"prompt": "Bonferroni with 10 tests at overall 5%?", "answer": "Test each at 0.005", "explain": "0.05/10 per-test alpha."},
   {"prompt": "Novelty effect definition?", "answer": "Early inflated response to change that fades", "explain": "Run to steady state."},
   {"prompt": "Pre-register what before launch?", "answer": "Primary metric, MDE, sample size, runtime", "explain": "Locks the design against p-hacking."}]}],
 "handsOnLab": {"id": "il26-m11-lab", "title": "End-to-end experiment analysis", "brief": "Analyze a provided A/B dataset: check randomization, compute the lift with CI, run guardrails, cut by segment, and write the ship/hold memo.",
  "steps": ["Verify randomization (SRM check: split ratio vs expected).", "Compute conversion lift, 95% CI, and p-value (two-proportion z).", "Check guardrail metrics for degradation.", "Cut results by 2 segments; note any heterogeneity (hypothesis only).", "Write the experiment memo with a SHIP/HOLD/ITERATE recommendation."],
  "deliverable": "Analysis notebook + experiment memo with recommendation.",
  "checkpoint": "SRM check passes; CI and p-value agree; the memo's recommendation follows from the numbers, not the narrative."},
 "mockTask": {"id": "il26-m11-mock", "type": "choice", "q": "Interview: 'Your A/B test shows +5% with p=0.04 after peeking daily for 3 weeks. Ship?'", "choices": ["Ship; p<0.05 is significant.", "Don't ship on this evidence: daily peeking inflates false positives; rerun with a fixed horizon or sequential design before deciding.", "Ship to 50% of users.", "Extend the test another month."], "answer": 1, "explain": "Three weeks of daily peeks makes p=0.04 nearly meaningless. The honest answer names the peeking problem and prescribes a valid design."},
 "crossLinks": [{"course": "il-15-data-engineering-analytics-interview-prep", "moduleId": "data-science-interview-prep--experimentation-ab", "topic": "Experimentation and A/B testing interview framing", "why": "Genuine A/B testing coverage pairing with the statistical machinery here."}],
 "videoPlaceholders": False}}

M12 = {"id": "IL26-M12", "patch": {
 "objectives": [
  "Design star and snowflake schemas: choose facts, dimensions, grain, and keys for a business process.",
  "Implement slowly changing dimensions (Types 1/2/3) and explain when each is correct.",
  "Build a metrics layer / semantic model: define each KPI once with its grain, filters, and owner.",
  "Evaluate normalization vs denormalization trade-offs and design for the warehouse (not OLTP)."],
 "notes": [
  {"title": "Facts, dimensions, and grain",
   "body": "A fact table holds measurable events at a declared grain ('one row per order line'). Dimensions hold descriptive context (who/what/when/where). The grain statement is the most important sentence in modeling: every ambiguity in a dashboard traces back to an unstated grain.\n\nWorked example (with solution):\n```text\nBusiness process: online orders.\nGrain: one row per order LINE ITEM (not per order: an order with 3 items\nmust be 3 rows, or item-level analysis is impossible).\nFacts: quantity, unit_price, discount, net_amount (additive), order_id (degenerate dimension).\nDimensions: dim_date, dim_customer, dim_product, dim_promotion.\nTest: 'Can I answer: revenue by product category by month?' Yes: join\nfacts to dim_product (category) and dim_date (month), sum net_amount.\nIf the grain were per-order, product-level questions would be unanswerable.\n```"},
  {"title": "Star vs snowflake",
   "body": "Star: denormalized dimensions (one dim_product with category columns). Snowflake: normalized dimensions (dim_product -> dim_category). Stars query simpler and faster (fewer joins); snowflakes save storage and enforce hierarchies. Modern warehouses favor stars — storage is cheap, analyst time is not.\n\nWorked example (with solution):\n```sql\n-- Star: single join to get category revenue.\nSELECT p.category, SUM(f.net_amount) AS revenue\nFROM fact_order_line f JOIN dim_product p ON f.product_key = p.product_key\nGROUP BY 1;\n-- Snowflake equivalent needs two joins (product -> subcategory -> category).\n-- Rule: snowflake only when a dimension hierarchy is maintained by a\n-- separate team/system as its own master data; otherwise denormalize.\n```"},
  {"title": "Slowly changing dimensions: Types 1, 2, 3",
   "body": "Type 1: overwrite (no history — 'current address only'). Type 2: new row per change with effective dates + current flag (full history — 'show revenue by customer's region AT ORDER TIME'). Type 3: keep current + one previous value as columns (limited history). Choose by the question history must answer.\n\nWorked example (with solution):\n```sql\n-- Type 2 dim_customer: same customer, two rows (one per address era).\n-- customer_key | customer_id | region | eff_from   | eff_to     | is_current\n-- 101          | 7           | West   | 2024-01-01 | 2026-03-14 | false\n-- 102          | 7           | East   | 2026-03-15 | 9999-12-31 | true\n-- Order on 2026-02-01 joins to key 101 (West): historical truth preserved.\n-- Join condition: f.order_date BETWEEN d.eff_from AND d.eff_to.\n-- Type 1 would have rewritten West->East and corrupted every historical report.\n```"},
  {"title": "The metrics layer: define once, use everywhere",
   "body": "A metrics layer (semantic model) defines each KPI once: its formula, grain, filters, and owner. Without it, five dashboards compute 'revenue' five ways. The layer sits between the warehouse and BI tools; every certified number comes from it.\n\nWorked example (with solution):\n```text\nMETRIC: net_revenue\n  formula: SUM(fact_order_line.net_amount) WHERE status = 'paid'\n  grain: order line; time spine: dim_date\n  owner: Finance Analytics; certified: 2026-09-15\n  includes: discounts, excludes: refunds (separate metric: refunds)\nGovernance rule: any dashboard titled with 'Revenue' must reference\nnet_revenue from the layer; custom calculations get a '_custom' suffix\nand cannot be called 'revenue'.\n```"},
  {"title": "Warehouse design: denormalize deliberately",
   "body": "OLTP normalizes to prevent update anomalies; warehouses denormalize for read speed and analyst sanity. Pre-join, pre-aggregate, and use wide tables where the query pattern is known. Surrogate keys (integers) join faster and survive source-system key changes.\n\nWorked example (with solution):\n```sql\n-- Wide daily mart: pre-joined, pre-aggregated for the dashboard's queries.\nCREATE TABLE mart_daily_sales AS\nSELECT d.date, p.category, c.region,\n       SUM(f.net_amount) AS revenue, COUNT(DISTINCT f.order_id) AS orders\nFROM fact_order_line f\nJOIN dim_date d ON f.date_key = d.date_key\nJOIN dim_product p ON f.product_key = p.product_key\nJOIN dim_customer c ON f.customer_key = c.customer_key\nGROUP BY 1, 2, 3;\n-- The dashboard hits this 4-column mart, not 4 joins: fast and foolproof.\n-- Surrogate keys (date_key, product_key) insulate the model from source\n-- natural-key changes (e.g., product SKU renumbering).\n```"}],
 "cards": [
  {"front": "Grain statement for an order fact table?", "back": "One row per order LINE ITEM — declared before anything else."},
  {"front": "Star vs snowflake: when snowflake?", "back": "Only when a hierarchy is mastered by a separate system; otherwise denormalize."},
  {"front": "SCD Type 1 vs 2 vs 3?", "back": "T1 overwrite (no history); T2 new row per change with dates (full history); T3 current + one previous column."},
  {"front": "Type 2 join condition?", "back": "fact.date BETWEEN dim.eff_from AND dim.eff_to."},
  {"front": "Metrics layer in one sentence?", "back": "Each KPI defined once (formula, grain, filters, owner); all certified numbers come from it."},
  {"front": "Why surrogate keys?", "back": "Fast integer joins; survive source natural-key changes."},
  {"front": "Degenerate dimension example?", "back": "order_id stored directly on the fact (no separate dimension needed)."},
  {"front": "Warehouse vs OLTP modeling?", "back": "Warehouses denormalize for reads; OLTP normalizes to prevent update anomalies."}],
 "quiz": [
  {"q": "Grain: 'one row per order line item' enables which analysis?", "choices": ["Product-level revenue analysis", "Only daily totals", "Customer counts only", "Nothing extra"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Line-item grain preserves product detail; coarser grains would lose it."},
  {"q": "Snowflake schemas are preferred when:", "choices": ["Always", "Never", "A hierarchy is mastered by a separate system as its own master data", "For speed"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Snowflaking adds joins; justify it only with separately-mastered hierarchies."},
  {"q": "Customer moved West->East; reports must show historical orders under West. SCD type?", "choices": ["Type 1", "Type 2", "Type 3", "None"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Type 2 keeps full history with effective dates; Type 1 would overwrite and corrupt history."},
  {"q": "Type 2 dimension join condition?", "choices": ["Equality on customer_id only", "fact.date BETWEEN eff_from AND eff_to", "is_current = true always", "No join needed"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Historical facts join to the version effective at the fact's date."},
  {"q": "Five dashboards compute 'revenue' five ways. The fix?", "choices": ["More dashboards", "A metrics layer: define revenue once with formula, grain, filters, owner", "Bigger warehouse", "Faster BI tool"], "answer": 2, "explain": "One certified definition consumed everywhere ends metric drift."},
  {"q": "Why use surrogate integer keys?", "choices": ["They look nicer", "Fast joins and insulation from source natural-key changes", "They save no space", "Required by law"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Integer joins are fast; surrogates survive SKU renumbering and source merges."},
  {"q": "Account balances are which fact type?", "choices": ["Semi-additive (sum across customers, not across time)", "Additive", "Non-additive", "Text"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Balances sum across customers but not across days (double-counts time)."},
  {"q": "Role-playing dimension example?", "choices": ["Two fact tables", "A junk dimension", "A snowflake", "dim_date joined as both order date and ship date"], "answer": 3, "explain": "One physical dim_date plays two logical roles via aliased joins."}],
 "drills": [{"id": "il26-m12-model", "title": "Data modeling drills", "items": [
   {"prompt": "Fact vs dimension?", "answer": "Facts: measurable events. Dimensions: descriptive context.", "explain": "The core modeling split."},
   {"prompt": "Additive vs semi-additive fact?", "answer": "Additive sums across all dims (revenue); semi-additive not across time (balances)", "explain": "Balances sum across customers but not across days."},
   {"prompt": "SCD Type 2 needs which columns?", "answer": "Surrogate key, effective dates, current flag (and the natural key)", "explain": "Versioning machinery."},
   {"prompt": "Conformed dimension?", "answer": "Shared across fact tables (e.g., one dim_date for all marts)", "explain": "Enables cross-process analysis."},
   {"prompt": "Junk dimension?", "answer": "Groups low-cardinality flags into one dimension", "explain": "Avoids a dozen tiny dimensions."},
   {"prompt": "Role-playing dimension?", "answer": "Same dim_date joined twice as order date and ship date", "explain": "One physical table, multiple logical roles."}]}],
 "handsOnLab": {"id": "il26-m12-lab", "title": "Model a business from scratch", "brief": "Design a star schema for a subscription business: grain statements, fact/dimension tables, one Type 2 dimension, and a metrics-layer definition for MRR.",
  "steps": ["Write grain statements for 2 fact tables (subscriptions, payments).", "Design dimensions; mark one as Type 2 with effective-date columns.", "Draw the schema (tables, keys, relationships).", "Write the metrics-layer definition for MRR (formula, grain, filters, owner).", "Write 3 business questions and the SQL each needs; verify answerability."],
  "deliverable": "Schema diagram + DDL + MRR metric definition + 3 verified queries.",
  "checkpoint": "Every business question is answerable from the schema; the Type 2 join uses effective dates."},
 "mockTask": {"id": "il26-m12-mock", "type": "choice", "q": "Interview: 'Design analytics for a food-delivery app.' What do you clarify first?", "choices": ["The BI tool.", "The grain: what is one row of the central fact (order? order item? delivery attempt?) — everything follows from grain.", "The color scheme.", "The warehouse vendor."], "answer": 1, "explain": "Grain determines answerable questions. Tool/vendor/color choices are downstream of the model."},
 "crossLinks": [{"course": "il-15-data-engineering-analytics-interview-prep", "moduleId": "data-eng-interview-prep--data-modeling-warehousing", "topic": "Data modeling and warehousing", "why": "Existing module teaches dimensional modeling this module deepens for analysts."}],
 "videoPlaceholders": False}}

